  "query_preview": "..\nToday and Tonight: A very active late afternoon and evening is \nin store for the Inland NW with scattered severe thunderstorms \nproducing damaging winds, isolated large hail, and torrential \nrain. The Storm Prediction Center is carrying a slight risk for \nsevere thunderstorms. A deep low remains in place to our south \nacross central California and Nevada, with south-southeast flow \npersisting. A strong mid level wave associated with this low \nwill come up from the south late this afternoon into the \nevening. Prior to its arrival, some of the latest CAM's models \nare showing convection developing earlier this afternoon over \nthe ID Panhandle with the presence of both elevated and surface \nbased instability. A warm, muggy day with high temperatures warm\ninto the upper 80s to low 90s will combine with dewpoints in \nthe 50s (locally low to mid 60s) which will allow for high CAPE \n(Convective Available Potential Energy) to develop with the bulk\nof guidance showing 800-1500 J/kg, locally as high as 2000-3000\nJ/KG over SE Washington into the south ID Panhandle. This \ncombined with 0-6km shear values of 30-40 kts will lead to \nscattered severe thunderstorms to develop as that mid level wave\nmoves in late this afternoon into the evening. Prior to its \narrival, isolated stronger to severe storms have the potential \nto develop in the early afternoon hours in the ID Panhandle. The\nshear today will be favorable for long-lived storms, with CAM's\nmodels showing increasing thunderstorm activity Thursday \nevening, with some activity continuing into the overnight hours.\nThese high of CAPE values supports the potential for large \nhail. \n\nIn addition, 25-40 degree temperature dewpoint spreads will lead \nto enhanced downdrafts from storms, leading to a threat of \ndamaging wind gusts. In fact, several models suggest a line of \nstrong winds propagating out of the Lower Columbia Basin and \ntracking up to the Canadian border this evening with 40-60 MPH \ngusts, with isolated stronger gusts possible. Initially prior to \nthe rain arrival a significant amount of blowing dust has the \npotential to get picked up in the Columbia Basin this evening, and\nmay lead to impacts on portions of Interstate 90 and Highway 395 \naround the Moses Lake and Ritzville areas but this only carries \nlow to moderate confidence.\n\nFriday through Wednesday: An upper trough dropping towards the \nregion will finally eject the low to our south toward the \nnortheast, but south- southwest flow will result in another round \nof showers and thunderstorms as moisture and instability linger. \nAlthough increasing winds in Central WA will lead to the best \ninstability shifting to NE WA/ID Panhandle during the afternoon. \nOn Saturday as the trough pushes in drier, cooler, and more stable\nair enters. As the low to the south pushes east, there is a 20-30%\nchance that showers will wrap around the back side of the low over\nfar NE WA/N ID Panhandle Saturday, Sunday, and Monday. Otherwise\nweak high pressure is still shown by the ensembles during this\nperiod allowing for a warming trend. JW",
